## 2. Filter rules on HQ-Router

HQ-Router started with no filter rules, so it accepted everything. Two rules now protect the router from the ISP side (ether4):

| # | Chain | Match | Action |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | input | connection state: established, related | accept |
| 1 | input | in. interface: ether4 | drop |

Rule 0 must sit above rule 1. Replies to connections the router started are accepted first, and new connections arriving on ether4 fall through to the drop rule.

![HQ filter rules](../screenshots/44-hq-filter-rules-input.png)

![Rule 0 with established and related ticked](../screenshots/45-hq-filter-rule0-established-related.png)

### Tests

| Test | Result | Why |
|---|---|---|
| HQ-Router pings 10.10.10.1 (ISP-Sim) | Replies | HQ started it, so the replies are established and rule 0 accepts them |
| ISP-Sim pings 10.10.10.2 (HQ ether4) | No replies | A new connection arriving on ether4 is dropped by rule 1 |
| Rule 0 disabled, HQ-Router pings 10.10.10.1 | Timeouts | The replies arrive on ether4, match no accept rule and are dropped by rule 1 |
| Rule 0 enabled again | Replies | Replies match rule 0 again |

A disabled rule stays in the list but the router skips it.

