## 3. Source NAT (masquerade)

HQ-Router's existing masquerade rule was set to leave through **ether3**, left over from Module 3 when ether3 was the WAN. The link to ISP-Sim is now **ether4** (`10.10.10.2/30`), and ether3 has no address, so the rule never matched internet-bound traffic.

![Masquerade rule before the change, out interface ether3](../screenshots/46-hq-nat-masquerade-before.png)

![HQ addresses and interfaces: ether4 is the ISP link, ether3 has no address](../screenshots/48-hq-addresses-interfaces.png)

Rule settings:

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| Chain | srcnat |
| Out. Interface | ether4 |
| Action | masquerade |

![Masquerade action](../screenshots/47-hq-nat-masquerade-action.png)

### Why it is needed
ISP-Sim has no route to private networks like `192.168.20.0/24`. Masquerade replaces the private source address with ether4's own address (`10.10.10.2`), which ISP-Sim is directly attached to, so replies can come back. The router then swaps the address back for the original sender.

### Test
With the rule's counters reset:

```
/ping 10.10.10.1 src-address=192.168.20.1 count=5
```

The rule's counter went from 0 to **56 B / 1 packet**, confirming the rule matched.

![NAT rule counters before the test](../screenshots/49-hq-nat-rule-counters-before-test.png)

![NAT rule counters after the test](../screenshots/50-hq-nat-rule-counters-after-test.png)

