## 4. Destination NAT (publishing an inside service)

Goal: let the ISP side reach the web service on Branch-Router (`192.168.10.2`, `www` enabled on port 80) through HQ-Router's address.

Rule on HQ-Router:

| Tab | Field | Value |
|---|---|---|
| General | Chain | dstnat |
| General | Protocol | tcp |
| General | Dst. Port | 8080 |
| General | In. Interface | ether4 |
| Action | Action | dst-nat |
| Action | To Addresses | 192.168.10.2 |
| Action | To Ports | 80 |

Visitors use port **8080** on HQ's address. HQ-Router passes the connection to port **80** on Branch. Port 8080 was chosen to keep the two ports distinct and to avoid clashing with HQ's own web service on port 80.

![dstnat rule, General tab](../screenshots/51-hq-dstnat-rule-general.png)

![dstnat rule, Action tab](../screenshots/52-hq-dstnat-rule-action.png)

### Test
From ISP-Sim's console:

```
/tool fetch url=http://10.10.10.2:8080/
```

The status was **finished**, and the dstnat rule's counter showed **60 B / 1 packet** (the first packet of the connection).

![Fetch from ISP-Sim finished](../screenshots/53-isp-fetch-finished.png)

After the rewrite, this traffic is **forward** traffic (it passes through HQ-Router to Branch), so the input drop rule on ether4 does not block it.

