## 5. DMZ design

A server that outsiders can reach should not sit on the same network as internal systems or management interfaces. A DMZ is a separate zone for such servers.

| Direction | Allowed? |
|---|---|
| Internet to DMZ | Yes, only the published service |
| Internal to DMZ | Yes |
| DMZ to internal | **No** |

The dangerous direction is DMZ to internal: if the server is compromised, the attacker must not be able to reach the internal networks.

### Implemented
ether3, which had no address, was given `192.168.60.1/24` as the DMZ network. The connected route appears automatically but stays inactive (flags include **D**, **C** and **I**) until a device is connected, and it would disappear if the address were removed.

![DMZ address and connected route](../screenshots/54-hq-dmz-address-route.png)

### Designed, not yet tested
No server or test device exists on ether3 or on the department VLANs, so these rules are designed but have not been verified:

| # | Chain | Match | Action |
|---|---|---|---|
| A | forward | connection state: established, related | accept |
| B | forward | src. address 192.168.60.0/24, dst. address 192.168.0.0/16 | drop |

Rule A sits above rule B so replies to staff connections still work. A DMZ server can still reach the internet because rule B only matches destinations inside `192.168.0.0/16`.

Moving the dstnat rule to a DMZ server means changing only **To Addresses** on the Action tab to the server's address (for example `192.168.60.10`). The match settings on the General tab stay the same. This was not applied, so the working test to Branch stays intact.

The same pattern (accept established/related, then drop the new connections) also works between VLANs, for example to stop the Guest VLAN (`192.168.40.0/24`) from starting connections to Finance (`192.168.20.0/24`). That was explained but not configured.

