## 6. Troubleshooting and lessons learned

| Issue | What happened | Lesson |
|---|---|---|
| Typo in a ping target | `ping 101.10.10.1` returned "net unreachable" | Check the address before blaming the firewall |
| Masquerade on the wrong interface | The rule named ether3, but internet traffic leaves through ether4 | Masquerade only touches packets leaving through the interface named in the rule |
| Counter showed 1 for five pings | NAT rules only see the first packet of a connection | Check counters after resetting them, and expect low numbers |
| Winbox lockout risk | Dropping all input on ether4 could cut off an IP-based session | Connecting by MAC address is not affected by IP firewall rules, and Safe Mode adds protection |
| Plain drop rules break replies | A drop rule between zones also drops replies | Put an established/related accept rule above the drop |

---

## 7. Summary
- Three chains: input (to the router), forward (through it), output (from it).
- Rules are checked top to bottom, and the first match wins; with no rules everything is accepted.
- An established/related accept rule above a drop rule keeps replies working.
- Masquerade (srcnat) rewrites the source of outgoing traffic so replies can return.
- dstnat rewrites the destination of incoming traffic so outsiders can reach an inside service.
- A DMZ keeps public servers in their own zone, with DMZ-to-internal traffic blocked.

